![Vector diagram](images/45/459c6127554e047ed5a7bb0500e73482ef0bf99fbd8b19e2506d5229c494032b.png)

Given that $\mathbf{c}=\lambda\mathbf{a}+\mu\mathbf{b}$, find $\lambda$ and $\mu$.

$\lambda=$ {{field-1}}

$\mu=$ {{field-2}}"] [:db/add "2e859a9d-08b6-5a6a-92af-dfb0da06cafb" :question/worked-solution "![Completed vector route](images/22/22dc092e8fed8174cf9862d441a63cad22b2f1430845f6d91eca795811a12358.png)

Construct the route $O\to X\to C$, first parallel to $\mathbf{a}$ and then parallel to $\mathbf{b}$.

Along $OX$, move $6$ squares down. A copy of $\mathbf{a}$ moves $4$ squares up. Thus $OX=\frac{3}{2}|\mathbf{a}|$ and the segment points in the opposite direction to $\mathbf{a}$. Hence $\overrightarrow{OX}=-\frac{3}{2}\mathbf{a}$.

Along $XC$, move $2$ squares left and $2$ squares up. A copy of $\mathbf{b}$ moves $2$ squares left and $2$ squares up. Thus $XC=1|\mathbf{b}|$ and the segment points in the same direction to $\mathbf{b}$. Hence $\overrightarrow{XC}=\mathbf{b}$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{c}&=\overrightarrow{OX}+\overrightarrow{XC}\\&=-\frac{3}{2}\mathbf{a}+\mathbf{b}
\end{aligned}
$$

